\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.0.1}{\ignorespaces The heavy-light cut of \(T\). \textbf  {Solid blue} edges are \emph  {heavy} (a node's largest-subtree child inherits its chain); \textbf  {dashed orange} edges are \emph  {light} (\(1\!\to \!2,2\!\to \!4,3\!\to \!6\)). Colour = chain membership: \(C_1=\{1,3,7,9,10\}\) is the heavy spine from the root, \(C_2=\{2,5,8\}\) hangs off the light edge \(1\!\to \!2\), and \(C_4=\{4\}, C_6=\{6\}\) are singletons off \(2\!\to \!4,3\!\to \!6\). Every root-to-node path crosses at most \(\lfloor \log _2 10\rfloor =3\) light edges (Theorem~\ref {thm:c16-lightedge}); the path \(1\!\to \!4\) meets \(1\!\to \!2\) and \(2\!\to \!4\) -- two of them. Node values \(v=(3,2,4,5,1,1,5,3,7,0)\).}}{1351}{figure.caption.243}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c16-chains}{{22.0.1}{1351}{The heavy-light cut of \(T\). \textbf {Solid blue} edges are \emph {heavy} (a node's largest-subtree child inherits its chain); \textbf {dashed orange} edges are \emph {light} (\(1\!\to \!2,2\!\to \!4,3\!\to \!6\)). Colour = chain membership: \(C_1=\{1,3,7,9,10\}\) is the heavy spine from the root, \(C_2=\{2,5,8\}\) hangs off the light edge \(1\!\to \!2\), and \(C_4=\{4\}, C_6=\{6\}\) are singletons off \(2\!\to \!4,3\!\to \!6\). Every root-to-node path crosses at most \(\lfloor \log _2 10\rfloor =3\) light edges (\thmref {c16-lightedge}); the path \(1\!\to \!4\) meets \(1\!\to \!2\) and \(2\!\to \!4\) -- two of them. Node values \(v=(3,2,4,5,1,1,5,3,7,0)\)}{figure.caption.243}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.0.2}{\ignorespaces The heavy-first \(\mathit  {pos}\) order and the value array \(B\) a segment tree is built on. Top row = position index; middle row = the node sitting at that position (\(1,3,7,9,10,6,2,5,8,4\)); bottom row = \(B[\mathit  {pos}]=v(\text  {node}) = (3,4,5,7,0,1,2,1,3,5)\), sum \(31\). A chain is one contiguous run: \(C_1=[1..5]\) (blue), \(C_2=[7..9]\) (green), \(C_6=[6..6]\) and \(C_4=[10..10]\) (singleton red). Crucially \emph  {subtrees stay intervals} too: \(\text  {subtree}(3)=\{3,6,7,9,10\}=[2..6]\) and \(\text  {subtree}(2)=\{2,4,5,8\} =[7..10]\) -- so \(\mathit  {pos}\) answers paths \emph  {and} subtrees for free. This is \(\mathit  {pos}=(1,7,2,10,8,6,3,9,4,5)\) for nodes \(1..10\).}}{1352}{figure.caption.244}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c16-pos}{{22.0.2}{1352}{The heavy-first \(\mathit {pos}\) order and the value array \(B\) a segment tree is built on. Top row = position index; middle row = the node sitting at that position (\(1,3,7,9,10,6,2,5,8,4\)); bottom row = \(B[\mathit {pos}]=v(\text {node}) = (3,4,5,7,0,1,2,1,3,5)\), sum \(31\). A chain is one contiguous run: \(C_1=[1..5]\) (blue), \(C_2=[7..9]\) (green), \(C_6=[6..6]\) and \(C_4=[10..10]\) (singleton red). Crucially \emph {subtrees stay intervals} too: \(\text {subtree}(3)=\{3,6,7,9,10\}=[2..6]\) and \(\text {subtree}(2)=\{2,4,5,8\} =[7..10]\) -- so \(\mathit {pos}\) answers paths \emph {and} subtrees for free. This is \(\mathit {pos}=(1,7,2,10,8,6,3,9,4,5)\) for nodes \(1..10\)}{figure.caption.244}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.0.3}{\ignorespaces The chapter's six-state taxonomy, read top to bottom as an escalation: \(d s1\) sizes the subtrees and picks the heavy child; \(d s2\) lays them on one line (\(\mathit  {pos}\)); the query walks heads; the update lays a lazy tag on each hop; subtree and path share the single order; and the light-edge lemma sets the cost and the chapter's boundary. Every solved problem sits on one row: SPOJ QTREE3 on rows~2--3, SPOJ QTREE on rows~3 and~5 (edge reduction), CF~593D on rows~3--4, CF~916E on row~5, and the Summit spanning rows~3--4.}}{1353}{figure.caption.245}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c16-taxonomy}{{22.0.3}{1353}{The chapter's six-state taxonomy, read top to bottom as an escalation: \(d s1\) sizes the subtrees and picks the heavy child; \(d s2\) lays them on one line (\(\mathit {pos}\)); the query walks heads; the update lays a lazy tag on each hop; subtree and path share the single order; and the light-edge lemma sets the cost and the chapter's boundary. Every solved problem sits on one row: SPOJ QTREE3 on rows~2--3, SPOJ QTREE on rows~3 and~5 (edge reduction), CF~593D on rows~3--4, CF~916E on row~5, and the Summit spanning rows~3--4}{figure.caption.245}{}}
