\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.1.1}{\ignorespaces The doubling step. At \(v\) the light child \(\ell \) is no bigger than the heavy child \(c\); together with \(v\) they account for the whole subtree, so \(\mathit  {size}(\ell )\) is at most about half of \(\mathit  {size}(v)\). Walking to \(\ell \), the subtree you now stand above more than doubles -- it cannot keep doubling past \(n\).}}{1357}{figure.caption.246}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c16-lightedge}{{22.1.1}{1357}{The doubling step. At \(v\) the light child \(\ell \) is no bigger than the heavy child \(c\); together with \(v\) they account for the whole subtree, so \(\mathit {size}(\ell )\) is at most about half of \(\mathit {size}(v)\). Walking to \(\ell \), the subtree you now stand above more than doubles -- it cannot keep doubling past \(n\)}{figure.caption.246}{}}
