\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.2.1}{\ignorespaces Path \(4\)--\(10\) climbed by \(Definition~\ref {def:c16-jump}\). \(u=4\) (head~4) and \(v=10\) (head~1): head~4 is deeper, fold \([\mathit  {pos}(4),\mathit  {pos}(4)]=[10,10]\), hop \(u\) to parent\((4)=2\); head~2 deeper than head~1, fold \([7,7]\), hop \(u\) to parent\((2)=1\); now both heads are~1, fold the same-chain span \([\mathit  {pos}(1),\mathit  {pos}(10)]=[1,5]\). The final meeting node~\(1\) \emph  {is} \(\mathrm  {lca}(4,10)\).}}{1364}{figure.caption.247}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c16-jump}{{22.2.1}{1364}{Path \(4\)--\(10\) climbed by \(\defref {c16-jump}\). \(u=4\) (head~4) and \(v=10\) (head~1): head~4 is deeper, fold \([\mathit {pos}(4),\mathit {pos}(4)]=[10,10]\), hop \(u\) to parent\((4)=2\); head~2 deeper than head~1, fold \([7,7]\), hop \(u\) to parent\((2)=1\); now both heads are~1, fold the same-chain span \([\mathit {pos}(1),\mathit {pos}(10)]=[1,5]\). The final meeting node~\(1\) \emph {is} \(\mathrm {lca}(4,10)\)}{figure.caption.247}{}}
