\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.4.1}{\ignorespaces The plain \(\tin  \) order and the HLD \(\mathit  {pos}\) order of \(T\) side by side (cells show the node stored at each position; colour \(=\) chain). Both are DFS preorders, so both keep every subtree contiguous -- but they assign \emph  {different coordinates} to the same subtree: subtree~2 is \([2..5]\) under \(\tin  \) and \([7..10]\) under \(\mathit  {pos}\), subtree~3 is \([6..10]\) under \(\tin  \) and \([2..6]\) under \(\mathit  {pos}\). Only \(\mathit  {pos}\) additionally makes each chain one block (\(C_1=[1..5]\)). Any structure must commit to ONE of the two coordinate systems; ranges taken from the other are silently wrong.}}{1376}{figure.caption.248}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c16-orders}{{22.4.1}{1376}{The plain \(\tin \) order and the HLD \(\mathit {pos}\) order of \(T\) side by side (cells show the node stored at each position; colour \(=\) chain). Both are DFS preorders, so both keep every subtree contiguous -- but they assign \emph {different coordinates} to the same subtree: subtree~2 is \([2..5]\) under \(\tin \) and \([7..10]\) under \(\mathit {pos}\), subtree~3 is \([6..10]\) under \(\tin \) and \([2..6]\) under \(\mathit {pos}\). Only \(\mathit {pos}\) additionally makes each chain one block (\(C_1=[1..5]\)). Any structure must commit to ONE of the two coordinate systems; ranges taken from the other are silently wrong}{figure.caption.248}{}}
